Good:
  if (isVisible(ln.charAt(col - 1))){ return new Pos(line, col); }
  int spaces = tabWidth - ((col - 1) % tabWidth);
Explanation: Message only renders sources checked against Fs.allowed (printable ASCII, space, newline), and tokenizing stops at the first character outside it. No tab and no character outside the BMP ever comes before a reported position, so a column is a char index and tab stops before it never happen.

Good:
  return new Span(fileName,first.line(),first.column(),l,Math.max(minCol,c - 1));
  base= new Span(fileName,preLine,preCol,line,col);
Explanation: a span whose last token ends with a newline ends at column 1 of the next line, and the file span ends one past the last character. The only Fearless tokens that can end with a newline are white space and an unclosed block comment, and their error messages look the same with or without that final newline.

Good:
  public Optional<T> peekLast(){ return peekAbs(limit-1); }
Explanation: the back end of a MetaParser is consumed only as the closing half of an opening token consumed from the front (expect(open); expectLast(close)), on groups the token tree builds only when both are present, or before anything is consumed from the front. The last token is never already consumed from the front.

Good:
  public Span spanAround(int low, int high){ ... span(ts.get(low),ts.get(high)) ... }
Explanation: a MetaParser span of a token range is taken from its two end tokens. Only skip tokens could make that wrong, and the Fearless parser skips only _SOF and _EOF, which parseFileFull consumes before any span is taken. White space never reaches the parser: postTokenize removes it.

Good:
  Message renders a caret only inside the visible text of a line; a span starting one column past the end of a line gets no caret.
Explanation: the Frontend never reports such a span. End of file and missing closer errors point at a real token (the unclosed opener, or the token found instead), so every reported position is inside a line's text.

Good:
  int l= 1, c= 1;
Explanation: MetaTokenizer.whiteList counts positions from 1:1, not from startingPosition. The only tokenizer, Frontend's Parse, starts at 1:1.

Good:
  <comment>%s</comment>  (LinuxAssociations.mimePackage, identity not XML escaped)
Explanation: the identity is the launcher file name chosen by the build (fearless...), and extensions and icon names are generated. None of them can hold XML special characters.
